<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kailasanathar Temple, Kanchipuram</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 700</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Kailasanatha Temple, Kanchipuram</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kailasanathar Temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, was built around 700 by the Pallava king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narasimhavarman-ii">Narasimhavarman II</a>, also called Rajasimha. Dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, it is the oldest structural temple in the city and a key early work of the southern Dravida style. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> drew on it for the Virupaksha temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pattadakal">Pattadakal</a> after taking Kanchipuram.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kailasanathar-temple-kanchipuram.jpg" alt="Kailasanathar Temple, Kanchipuram" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bikash Das from bangalore, india · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:7th_century_Sri_Kailashnathar_Temple_Kanchipuram_Tamil_Nadu_India_01_(11).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
